  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on outcomes with probabilities less than 20%, adjusting their weights upward by 10 percentage points (i.e., adding 0.10 to their objective probability) and then evaluating each lottery based on the weighted sum of these low-probability outcomes. In the given decision problem, Lottery A has no outcome with a probability below 20% (its probabilities are 25%, 25%, and 50%), and Lottery B also has no outcome with a probability below 20% (25% and 75%). Therefore, strictly speaking, there are no outcomes with probability less than 20%, so the adjustment rule (increasing weight by 10 percentage points) does not apply to any outcome. As a result, the decision reason cannot be meaningfully applied because its core condition (existence of <20% probability outcomes) is not satisfied. However, if one interprets 'less than 20%' inclusively or with rounding, or considers proximity to 20%, the 25% outcomes might be borderline considered — but this would be inconsistent with the strict definition. Thus, the reason is not applicable to this decision problem as defined.",
